  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA complex low pressure system will impact the area today into\nSunday. Multiple low pressure systems currently reside along a\nstalled boundary stretching from the Gulf to east of the \nBahamas early this morning. Shortwave energy lifting out of the\nGulf will aid in lifting the low pressure system northward later\ntoday through Sunday. Most guidance continues to converge on a \nsolution of the low east of the Bahamas becoming the dominate \nlow as it lift northward off the coast. However, some models \nsolutions continue to show the primary low remaining closer to \nthe Southeast coast and tracking across ENC. While this\ncontinues to bring some uncertainty with the details of the\nforecast, there is not significant changes with the latest\nforecast package. \n\nThe greatest impacts with this system are expected along the\ncoast where large surf, beach erosion, and possible minor ocean\noverwash will be possible with the contribution of high \nastronomical tides adding to the impacts. Still think coastal \nflooding impacts across the sounds and rivers will be minimal\ngiving the weaker winds, but given the developing King Tides, \nthere may be minor inundation for locations adjacent to the \nsounds and rivers.\n\nRainfall amounts continue to trend lower as ENC looks to remain\nbetween the two areas of ascent with much of ENC now only \nforecast to see 0.25-0.75\" of rain this weekend from these \nsystems. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The King Tide Cycle starts second half of this \nweekend, peaking middle of next week. This higher than normal \nastronomical tide cycle is slightly stronger than the one that \noccurred the last week of OCT. The tide levels may be \nexacerbated by a series of low pressure systems that are \nexpected to impact the region over the next week. \n\nThe first system will impact the area this weekend, however \nguidance has trended weaker with the winds and resulting in \nminimal impacts expected for most areas, however this system \nwill produce large surf along the coast and the higher than \nnormal tides may help contribute to ocean overwash around times\nof high tide, especially where the dune structure is\ncompromised. \n\nAnother potentially stronger low pressure system is expected to\nimpact the area around the middle of next week. The strength \nand track of this low will determine where the greatest impacts\nwill occur, but most tidal locations across ENC should expect \nelevated water levels with at least minor impacts across the \ntypical vulnerable areas. Interests along the coast should watch\nthe forecast closely over the next week for the potential for \ngreater impacts, especially as it has to potential to impact\nholiday travel.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...In the wake of this weekend's low, strong high \nbuilds in from the north early next week. Models continue to \nshow a deepening trough moving through the Gulf states and \nprogressing offshore of the SE US with a surface low developing\nalong the coastal trough located near the Gulf Stream, then\ndeepening as it lifts northward off the coast Wednesday into\nThursday. Upper level support is greater with this system and \nhigh pressure to the north will be stronger which will likely\nbring a tighter pressure gradient across the area resulting in \nstronger winds. While there remains considerable uncertainty \nwith the details with this system, it will have the potential \nto bring greater impacts across the area, especially along the \ncoast and tidal locations as it will coincide with the peak of\nKing Tides bringing a greater risk of coastal flooding, beach\nerosion, and ocean overwash. This system will also have the \npotential to bring impacts to holiday travel on Wednesday and \nThursday.",
